From 210b6c74b78daea85b534021210c0b97adc07aef Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Micheal Waltz <micheal@ecliptik.com>
Date: Wed, 23 Sep 2026 10:39:49 -0700
Subject: [PATCH] [SDL3-DOS] dos: patch 0144 -- deferred MIDI port writes,
 replayed on time by the SB IRQ (opt-in)

WHY DOS NEEDS THIS: a port that runs its MIDI scheduler from the main
loop gets note timing quantised to the render rate -- every event due
between two polls goes out in one batch at the next poll -- and frozen
through any main-loop stall. dosags (AGS) measured it on real hardware
(PLAN.md, 8.3 re-run SB4O/SB4W, 2026-09-23): the 640x480 intro renders at
~6 fps on the 486 and 72% of MIDI events went out more than 50 ms late in
regular 150-200 ms bursts, heard as the music "speeding up randomly",
while overall tempo was right. doskutsu solved the same problem with
SDL/0072 by running its own fixed-point scheduler inside the SB IRQ. A
port that uses the hub's shared midi_sched + opl3_sink/waveblaster_sink
cannot: midi_sched_tick uses the FPU, the sinks allocate at open and keep
unlocked state -- none of it may run in an ISR.

WHAT: the port keeps its scheduler and sinks on the main thread,
unchanged, but runs them AHEAD, one IRQ slot at a time; the SB ISR plays
the result at the right IRQ.
  - SDL_DOSMidiDeferEnable(kind) (SDL_dosmidi_tick.h): 1 = OPL3 register
    writes (SDL_DOSOpl3WriteRegister), 2 = MPU-401 bytes
    (SDL_DOSMpu401WriteByte). Kind 2 only on the blind direct-port
    transport: the SDL/0093 DRR status read (ISA IOCHRDY can stall the
    CPU), the SDL/0098 TX-delay PIT busy-wait and the DSP-mediated path are
    refused, as is a closed SB device.
  - While enabled, each write of that kind is captured into a fixed
    8192-entry queue stamped with SDL_DOSMidiDeferSetStamp's IRQ index
    (SDL_DOSMidiDeferIrqNow = dos_port_audio_irq_count: one IRQ = one DMA
    half-buffer, the device's own clock).
  - Every SB IRQ, after the existing counters and before the SDL/0072
    tick, writes out the queue head while its stamp is due, at most 256
    entries per IRQ.
  - Stats: SDL_DOSMidiDeferStats (written, written >= 1 IRQ late, most
    IRQs late, most in one IRQ, captures refused because the queue was
    full); SDL_DOSMidiDeferStats2 (discarded unplayed, written by a
    disable).

ISR constraints, as met by the code:
  - The ISR path (MidiDeferDrain, MidiDeferWriteOne) is integer ops and
    inportb/outportb only: no malloc/free, no printf/log, no FPU, no uclock,
    no SDL call.
  - Single producer (main thread) / single consumer (ISR). The entry is
    stored, then a compiler barrier, then the tail moves; the ISR reads the
    entry after seeing the tail.
  - Locked memory: the queue arrays and their indices are DPMI-locked at
    the first enable; g_mdq_kind/g_mdq_hold at OpenDevice with the ISR's
    other variables, because a port's own ISR (the 0072 tick) reaches the
    write primitives that test them.
  - The drain code's lock is DOS_LockCode(MidiDeferWriteOne,
    MidiDeferDrain_End). That relies on source-order function layout, which
    DJGPP gcc 12 -O2 does not keep: in the built object MidiDeferDrain is
    inlined into SoundBlasterIRQHandler, whose own range is already
    negative in this series. The next patch (0145) replaces that
    assumption for every ISR.

Ordering and bounds:
  - SDL_DOSOpl3Detect/InitChip/Shutdown HOLD the deferral while they run:
    their writes go direct and the ISR writes nothing, so it cannot
    interleave with their address/data writes. This holds even across
    Detect's SDL_DelayPrecise: that ends in DOS_Yield, and the thread
    switch's longjmp restores the other thread's EFLAGS (DJGPP's longjmp
    ends in iret), so the cli a first version relied on did not hold. With
    deferral off, Hold(true) is a no-op and the three run as before.
  - Enable(0) stops the ISR consuming, then writes out at most 1024 queued
    entries from the caller in batches of 32, with interrupts off only
    around each batch, and discards the rest, counted.
  - CloseDevice with deferral on stops it (later writes go direct) and
    discards the queue, counted.

Cost for a port that never calls SDL_DOSMidiDeferEnable (doskutsu's
SDL/0072 path, every SDL_mixer port):
  - one test of a locked byte per IRQ (g_mdq_kind != 0, false);
  - per OPL register write / MPU byte, including the ones its own
    SDL/0072 ISR callback makes: the inline test
    `g_mdq_kind == k && !g_mdq_hold && ...` on two locked bytes. The
    dosags message counts this as two tests; with deferral off,
    g_mdq_kind == k is false and short-circuits, so one byte is read
    and the capture call is never reached;
  - one SDL_DOSMidiDeferHold call per Detect/InitChip/Shutdown, returning
    false without touching anything.
The SDL/0072 tick itself (hook, callback, now_ms) is not touched.

Validation: DOSBox-X only; hardware confirmation pending.
  - Hub scratch probe, dosbox-run.sh --sb-live, a conf identical to
    dosbox-x-fast.conf but oplmode=opl3, device 11025 Hz via
    SDL_DOSAudioSetDeviceFrameRate (512-frame IRQs, 46.44 ms):
      A, 86 slots x 2 OPL writes, 43-IRQ lookahead, SDL_Delay(1500)
        mid-way: 64 written during the stall with 64 due, 172/172
        written, 0 late, burst_max 2.
      B, same without the stall: 172/172, 0 late.
      C, control on the same build, deferred API never called: all
        deferral counters 0; an SDL/0072 tick callback writing an OPL
        register from the IRQ gives 64 callbacks / 16 ISR OPL writes in
        65 IRQs, the same as the 0143 build.
      D, 300 Detect calls with deferral on and 800 writes queued: 30
        IRQs fired inside a Detect, 0 deferred writes inside; 300/300
        detected. The first version of this patch: 28 IRQs and 56 writes
        inside.
  - dosags (PLAN.md "MIDI TIMING FIX RESULT", ags-0207-clean
    7d43b8e155ed, cells MISI4/MITI4 vs MISP4/MITP4), 5 Days a Stranger,
    OPL3:
      1.5 s stalls every 150 ticks: late_max_ms 0, 0 of 22468 writes late,
        the ISR kept writing through every stall; the poll control reached
        1560 ms.
      ~6 fps render throttle: 0 of 27112 writes late; the poll control
        had 9583 of 11686 events more than 50 ms late.
  - OPL3 only. WaveBlaster stays on the main-loop poll, by design, and
    the WB ISR path is NOT demonstrated:
      - the cold-init MPU transport paces each byte with the SDL/0098
        320 us busy-wait (the DreamBlaster input-overrun fix; this
        series' code comment: "freeze-proof + fixes H20 garble");
      - a busy-wait like that has no place in an ISR, so kind 2 accepts
        only the blind transport (no status read, no delay), which is
        the configuration that garbles on the DreamBlaster;
      - dosags' WB cells (MIWI) therefore ran on the poll, and no run
        here or in dosags exercised kind 2 in the ISR.
  - KNOWN LIMITATION: in ISR mode a port that advances song time by
    the nominal IRQ period follows the card's actual playback rate.
    (Corrected 2026-09-23: this first said 0.988-0.996 on DOSBox-X and
    about -0.33% on the PicoGUS. Both were wrong. The DOSBox-X 0.988 was
    an error in dosags' own witness, which truncated each gap to whole
    ms. DOSBox-X's measured period is nominal, 46436-46447 us. On real
    hardware the PicoGUS in SB 2.0 mode at 11025 Hz measured 46127 us
    against 46439 nominal, 0.68% fast (dosags SB5O). A port should
    advance song time by the period measured against uclock, as dosags
    ags 0208 does. With that, OPL3 on the 486 read 0 of 12233 writes
    late at a 6 fps render rate, with the song clock 0.998 against the
    RTC.)

Source: dosags patches/SDL/0193 (From 097def7cde2c, committed in dosags
15d2149), re-derived against the hub series. One hunk's context is a
dosags-only patch (its 0141 uclock() write-cost diagnostic inside
SDL_DOSOpl3WriteRegister); the hub function has the same address/data
sequence without it, so the capture test sits at the top of the hub
function and nothing from 0141 is imported. Also changed from the dosags
file, all comments or declaration placement:
  - tags renumbered 0193 -> 0144;
  - the new declarations moved inside the header's extern "C" block,
    before its closing comment;
  - the header's stale "Enable(0) writes out whatever is still queued, at
    once" replaced by the bounded behaviour the code has;
  - the ISR write's comment no longer mentions a diagnostic this series
    does not have.

